#: ../../Clarification/Clarifier_Derivations.rst:198
msgid "Flocs that are rotating in a shear flow drag a boundary layer of fluid with them as they rotate. This boundary layer of fluid prevents any approaching fluid from penetrating to the surface of the floc. There is no stagnation point on the floc! This means that approaching particles are swept around the floc due to the presence of the boundary layer. The only way for a particle to collide with a large floc is for the particle to be large enough that it can penetrate through the boundary layer even though the center of the particle continues to follow the streamline around the boundary layer of the floc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Clarification/Clarifier_Derivations.rst:200
msgid "Flocs with low fractal dimensions that are falling through a fluid that is not undergoing significant shear have a small amount of fluid passes directly through the floc where any particles in the flow can collide with particles that are held inside the floc. Thus the floc is the filter and the filter media is the particles that make up the floc. This is why we call it a floc filter. Particles are filtered by individual flocs and are retained inside the floc. As particles accumulate inside the floc the floc porosity decreases and the flow through the floc decreases. Eventually the floc becomes ineffective as a filter because its filtration capacity has been exhausted."
msgstr ""

